  "query_preview": "..Radar shows some lingering showers in Sonora and\nfar western Pima County. An outflow made it into Santa Cruz\nCounty a few hours ago, but has since sputtered out. Aloft,\nbroad high pressure extends from southern California into the\nGulf of Mexico. This will keep high temperatures 5-9 degrees\nabove normal today and Thursday. Expect major HeatRisk in the\nTucson metro area, San Pedro River Valley, the Upper Gila River\nValley, and eastern Pinal County. An Extreme Heat Warning is in\neffect for these areas through Thursday. \n\nThe high is centered over southern New Mexico which has led to \nsoutherly mid-level flow starting yesterday, continuing through\ntoday. This has allowed moisture to advect into Sonora and now \nsoutheast Arizona. GOES East total precipitable water shows PWAT\nvalues ranging from 1.5\" in western Pima County to 0.8\" along \nthe AZ/NM border. By the afternoon PWATs are expected to be a \nlittle over an inch from Tucson eastward. This will be enough \nmoisture for isolated to scattered shower and thunderstorm \nactivity. The 24/00Z HREF shows most activity staying south and \neast of Tucson this afternoon and evening. Storms won't produce \nmuch rain, but strong gusty outflows to 45 mph and lightning \nwill be a threat. Outflows in Cochise and southern Graham \ncounties could create areas of blowing dust. Thursday moisture \ndecreases a little, mainly in the mid-levels, as winds turn more\nwesterly. However there will still be enough moisture in place \nfor another round of isolated to scattered thunderstorms during \nthe afternoon and evening. The HREF is hinting that storms will \nbe capable of stronger outflow winds thanks to the punch of dry \nair and more upper level divergence from slightly stronger \nupper level winds. It shows most of Cochise and Graham Counties \nunder a 40 km neighborhood probability of 10 percent for 50 kt \nwinds late afternoon into the evening.\n\nFriday moisture continues to decrease as a strong Pacific upper\nlevel low moves onshore bringing dry westerly winds. There may\nbe enough lingering moisture for buildups in the White\nMountains. The core of the low moves through the Great Basin \nthis weekend bringing area wide breezy winds Friday through\nMonday. The strongest winds will likely occur Saturday and\nSunday. Fire weather conditions will be elevated each day\nFriday-Monday with Saturday and Sunday likely seeing localized\nareas of Red Flag conditions. Right now not enough confidence\nand a big enough area of concern to put up a Fire Weather Watch.\nTemperatures will be a couple degrees above normal Friday and\nSaturday then drop to around normal Sunday into early next week. \n\nNext week the upper air pattern becomes amplified over CONUS\nwith ridging over the eastern half and troughing over the west\ncoast. This will keep temperatures right around normal and put\nsoutheast Arizona under southerly mid-level flow. This could\nlead to another slight increase in moisture by mid next week.",
